Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis – HashMap
Instead of reading the file for every search, we can read it once and store valid records in a HashMap.

Example Java code:
HashMap<String, String[]> map = new HashMap<>();

// Read file once
try (BufferedReader r = Files.newBufferedReader(file)) {

    String line;

    while ((line = r.readLine()) != null) {

        String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);

        if (fields.length == 5) {
            map.put(fields[0], fields);
        }
    }
}

// Search pass
String[] result = map.get("P1003");

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result));


Complexity comparison
Operation	File scanning	HashMap
Initial loading	Not required	O(n)
One search	O(n)	O(1) average
q searches	O(q × n)	O(n + q) average
Extra memory	O(1) for streaming	O(n)
Best use	Small number of searches	Many repeated searches

Explanation:
- File scanning reads the file again for every search.
- HashMap reads the file once and stores records using pass IDs as keys.
- HashMap usually finds a pass in constant average time.
- However, HashMap requires additional memory to store all records.
- In the worst case, HashMap operations can take longer than O(1) due to hash collisions.
